/// Median absolute deviation.
///
/// The median of the absolute deviations from the median, `median(|x - median(x)|)`, times
/// `constant:`. The constant is 1.4826 unless given, which makes the result estimate the
/// standard deviation of a normal distribution, as R's `mad` does by default; `constant: 1`
/// gives the raw median deviation. A robust measure of spread: it is unchanged by a shift,
/// scaled by a positive factor, and moved by no single outlier. The medians are taken over the
/// values present, with the reducers' warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of
/// the Language Reference), once for each median; one value gives 0, and two give the constant
/// times half their distance.
///
/// data: The frame.
/// var: The variable.
/// constant: The factor the median deviation is multiplied by; 1.4826 unless given, 1 for the raw value.
/// returns: The scaled median absolute deviation, a Float, printed as `mean` prints.
/// details: The deviations are computed in one pass over the frame and their median taken by `median`, the midpoint of the two middle values for an even count; 1.4826 is 1 / Φ⁻¹(3/4) to four decimals, the value R uses, not the exact quantile.
/// reference: Hampel, F. R. (1974) The influence curve and its role in robust estimation, Journal of the American Statistical Association 69(346), 383–393.
/// reference: R Core Team, the `mad` function of the stats package.
/// missing values: The two medians are taken over the values present, with a warning for the rows skipped, once for each median. A variable with no value left gives `NA`.
/// coming from: d.mad(wage) | mad(d$wage, na.rm = TRUE) | no command of its own
/// coming from: d.mad(wage, constant: 1) | mad(d$wage, constant = 1, na.rm = TRUE) | egen m = mad(wage)
/// coming from: R's `mad` multiplies by 1.4826 unless told, as here; Stata's `egen` function `mad()` gives the raw median deviation, which is `constant: 1`.
/// related: For the distance between the quartiles, use `iqr`.
/// related: For the standard deviation, which the default constant makes this estimate under normality, use `sd`.
/// related: For the median itself, use `median`.
/// see also: median, iqr, sd, abs
/// example: The median absolute deviation of the wages, scaled to estimate a standard deviation; beside it the standard deviation, which the long right tail of wages makes larger.
/// > let wages = dataset(wages)
/// > wages.mad(wage)
/// > wages.sd(wage)
/// 11.4753
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 2)
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in deviation were skipped (line 2)
/// 14.0269
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 3)
/// example: The raw median deviation, with a constant of 1.
/// > wages.mad(wage, constant: 1)
/// 7.74
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in deviation were skipped (line 4)
fn mad(data: Frame, var: Var, constant: Float = 1.4826) -> Float {
    let centre = data.median(var)
    // The deviations are taken outside the frame, as a column: inside one of its methods a
    // name of this body could be a variable of the frame given.
    constant * frame(deviation = abs(data[name_of(var)] - centre)).median(deviation)
}
